On April 14, 2022, business magnate [[Elon Musk]] offered to purchase American [[social media]] company [[Twitter, Inc.]], for $43&nbsp;billion, after previously acquiring 9.1&nbsp;percent of the company's stock for $2.64&nbsp;billion, becoming its largest shareholder. Twitter <!-- This is needed as the event took place before April 14 -->had<!-- This is needed as the event took place before April 14 --> then invited Musk to join their [[board of directors]], which Musk at first accepted before subsequently declining. Twitter then announced a "[[Shareholder rights plan|poison pill]]" strategy the next day. On April 25, Twitter's board of directors unanimously accepted Musk's buyout offer of $44&nbsp;billion, with the company set to be [[Private company|privatized]].

Reception to the proposed buyout has been mixed. The buyout has received praise for Musk's planned reforms and vision for the company, while also drawing criticism over fears of a potential rise in [[disinformation]] and [[Online harassment|harassment]] on the platform. {{As of|2022|7}}, the acquisition is pending approval from regulators and shareholders.

